\subsection{Distribution Shift Boundaries}
\label{sec:failure_modes}

\looseness=-1 Three distribution shifts define operating boundaries: \textbf{(1)} input shift ($K{=}1$, 10.3\%): without argmin centering, correction is unreliable; \textbf{(2)} task shift: near-OOD is adversarial ($-$12\,pp), far-OOD neutral ($p{=}0.46$); \textbf{(3)} feature shift: loosening $\varepsilon$ amplifies noisy per-DOF covariance (perdof: ${\sim}$14\% $\to$ 0\%; Section~\ref{sec:magnitude_results}). All three share a mechanism: CRM degrades when inputs depart from training distribution; all Table~\ref{tab:main} results are in-distribution. Improvement concentrates on medium-difficulty tasks (Appendix~\ref{sec:per_task}); Plan~A runs 1.82$\times$ faster than $K{=}10$ with 0.28\,ms CRM overhead (Appendix~\ref{sec:latency}).

